- name: bin_to_sym
  id: epy_block
  parameters:
    _source_code: "# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later\n\"\"\"CSS DEMOD MAP + SEGMENT SPLITTER — what the\
      \ DECODED-vs-TRANSMITTED scope draws.\n\nTwo jobs, and the second one is why this block exists at all.\n\n\
      **1. The decode map.** The on-chip FFT16 emits its 16 bins in bit-reversed (DIF)\norder, so the winning bin\
      \ index ``i`` is not the symbol: the symbol is\n``s = brev4(i)`` — the 4-bit reversal of the index.\n\n**2.\
      \ The reference rides the SAME stream as the decode.** This is the\nload-bearing part. The shipped burst is\
      \ TWO segments through one chain —\nsegment A at +10 dB (which decodes exactly) and segment B at -10 dB (the\n\
      on-chip NEGATIVE CONTROL, which must not). Displaying that correctly is not a\nmatter of taste; two earlier\
      \ display defects made a perfect decode look broken:\n\n  * **PHASE DRIFT.** The old flowgraph drew the transmitted\
      \ reference from a\n    SEPARATE ``vector_source`` on channel 1 of the scope. That source free-runs\n    while\
      \ the chip's stream is gated by the simulator's batch turnaround —\n    measured at **+27.9 % more items over\
      \ the same run**. A QT time_sink pulls\n    the same count from both channels, so the red reference SLID against\
      \ the\n    blue decode. Once offset by as few as 3 items, **22 of segment A's 24\n    perfectly-decoded symbols\
      \ render as mismatches**. The plot smeared while the\n    chip was bit-exact.\n  * **A/B CONFLATION.** Even\
      \ at zero offset, segment B's intentional garbage was\n    drawn on the same axis as segment A's perfect lock,\
      \ with nothing to tell a\n    viewer which half was which. 17 of 50 plotted points disagreed *by design*\n\
      \    and the plot did not say so.\n\nThe cure for both: derive the reference HERE, from the item index of\
      \ the very\nstream being decoded, and split the two segments onto their OWN channels. The\nreference cannot\
      \ drift from the decode because it is generated per item from the\nsame counter, and segment A's lock is never\
      \ overplotted by segment B's collapse.\n\nFrame layout (one batch = ``n_words`` items, ``seg_words`` per segment)::\n\
      \n    word 0            the framing-latency word — carries no data symbol\n    word 1 .. n_data  symbol f\
      \ arrives in word f+1\n\nso a segment's reference trace is ``[BLANK] + preamble + message symbols``.\n\nOutputs\
      \ (all four are the SAME length and the SAME phase — plot them together):\n\n  out0  segment A decoded  (chip)\
      \          blank outside segment A\n  out1  segment A transmitted (reference)  blank outside segment A\n \
      \ out2  segment B decoded  (chip)          blank outside segment B\n  out3  segment B transmitted (reference)\
      \  blank outside segment B\n\n``BLANK`` is NaN: GNU Radio's time sink leaves a gap rather than drawing a\n\
      misleading 0 or -1, so each pair occupies only its own half of the sweep.\n\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom\
      \ gnuradio import gr\n\n# brev4: 4-bit reversal, the FFT16 DIF output-order map (index -> symbol).\n_BREV4\
      \ = [((i & 1) << 3) | ((i & 2) << 1) | ((i & 4) >> 1) | ((i & 8) >> 3)\n          for i in range(16)]\n\n\
      BLANK = float(\"nan\")\n\n\nclass blk(gr.sync_block):\n    \"\"\"n        = samples per chirp symbol == FFT\
      \ size == alphabet (16)\n    tx_syms     = the transmitted symbol sequence for ONE segment, on the output\n\
      \                  word grid (element 0 is the framing-latency word)\n    seg_words   = index words per segment\n\
      \    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, n=16, tx_syms=(), seg_words=25):\n        gr.sync_block.__init__(\n\
      \            self, name='CSS Bin -> Symbol + A/B split',\n            in_sig=[np.float32],\n            out_sig=[np.float32,\
      \ np.float32, np.float32, np.float32])\n        self.n = int(n)\n        self.seg_words = int(seg_words)\n\
      \        # The per-segment reference trace, on the output word grid. Element 0 is\n        # the framing-latency\
      \ word (no data symbol) and is drawn BLANK.\n        ref = [BLANK] + [float(s) for s in tx_syms]\n       \
      \ ref = ref[:self.seg_words]\n        ref += [BLANK] * (self.seg_words - len(ref))\n        self._ref = np.asarray(ref,\
      \ dtype=np.float32)\n        # Item counter modulo the whole two-segment batch — this is what keeps\n    \
      \    # the reference locked to the decode (see the module docstring).\n        self._k = 0\n\n    def work(self,\
      \ input_items, output_items):\n        x = input_items[0]\n        nout = len(x)\n        w = self.seg_words\n\
      \        period = 2 * w\n\n        idx = np.rint(x.astype(np.float64)).astype(np.int64) & 0xFFFF\n       \
      \ sym = np.full(nout, BLANK, dtype=np.float32)\n        good = (idx >= 0) & (idx < 16)\n        sym[good]\
      \ = [float(_BREV4[i]) for i in idx[good]]\n\n        # Position of every item within the two-segment batch.\n\
      \        pos = (np.arange(nout, dtype=np.int64) + self._k) % period\n        in_a = pos < w\n        in_b\
      \ = ~in_a\n        ref = self._ref[pos % w]\n\n        # A word the reference does not cover carries no data\
      \ symbol (word 0 is\n        # the framing-latency word). Blank the DECODE there too — plotting it\n     \
      \   # would put a lone unmatched point on an otherwise exact overlay and\n        # invite precisely the \"\
      one symbol is wrong\" question it is not.\n        drawn = ~np.isnan(ref)\n        sym = np.where(drawn, sym,\
      \ BLANK)\n\n        a_dec, a_ref, b_dec, b_ref = (output_items[i] for i in range(4))\n        a_dec[:nout]\
      \ = np.where(in_a, sym, BLANK)\n        a_ref[:nout] = np.where(in_a, ref, BLANK)\n        b_dec[:nout] =\
      \ np.where(in_b, sym, BLANK)\n        b_ref[:nout] = np.where(in_b, ref, BLANK)\n\n        self._k = (self._k\
      \ + nout) % period\n        return nout\n"
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: s = brev4(index) AND the A/B split. The transmitted reference is generated HERE, from the same stream's
      item index, so it CANNOT drift against the decode (a separate vector_source ran 27.9% fast and smeared the
      plot). Segment A and segment B get their own channel pair.
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    n: n_css
    _io_cache: ('CSS Bin -> Symbol + A/B split', 'blk', [('n', '16'), ('tx_syms', '()'), ('seg_words', '25')], [('0',
      'float', 1)], [('0', 'float', 1), ('1', 'float', 1), ('2', 'float', 1), ('3', 'float', 1)], 'n        = samples
      per chirp symbol == FFT size == alphabet (16)\n    tx_syms     = the transmitted symbol sequence for ONE segment,
      on the output\n                  word grid (element 0 is the framing-latency word)\n    seg_words   = index
      words per segment\n    ', ['n', 'seg_words'])
    tx_syms: tx_syms
    seg_words: seg_words
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 1760
    - 320
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: sym_scope
  id: qtgui_time_sink_x
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    autoscale: 'False'
    axislabels: 'True'
    comment: 'BOTH segments, each against its OWN reference, all four traces phase-locked to the chip stream. A:
      blue decoded sits exactly on the cyan reference. B: red decoded scatters off the orange reference - the on-chip
      negative control.'
    ctrlpanel: 'False'
    entags: 'True'
    grid: 'False'
    gui_hint: ''
    legend: 'True'
    name: '"DECODED vs TRANSMITTED - segment A (+10 dB) locks | segment B (-10 dB) collapses"'
    nconnections: '4'
    size: n_words
    srate: samp_rate
    stemplot: 'False'
    tr_chan: '0'
    tr_delay: '0'
    tr_level: '0.0'
    tr_mode: qtgui.TRIG_MODE_FREE
    tr_slope: qtgui.TRIG_SLOPE_POS
    tr_tag: '""'
    type: float
    update_time: '0.10'
    ylabel: symbol
    ymax: '16'
    ymin: '-1'
    yunit: '""'
    alpha1: '1.0'
    color1: blue
    label1: A (+10 dB) decoded - chip
    marker1: '0'
    style1: '0'
    alpha2: '1.0'
    color2: cyan
    label2: A (+10 dB) transmitted
    marker2: '1'
    style2: '0'
